==============================================================================
A3. The brief's finite example, and the exact scope of uninformativeness
==============================================================================
  w=(1): S=1 C=1 q_w=4 r_w=3 K=2  seeds<=15: [3, 7, 11, 15]
  next odd states m_1: [5, 11, 17, 23]
  following valuations a_2: [4, 1, 2, 1]   cap K=2  -> failures at t = [0]
  m_1 mod 5: [0, 1, 2, 3]   -> 'm_1 = 0 mod 5' selects t = [0]
  => the example is CORRECT: an odd-modulus feature identifies the failure here.

  WHY, and what the correct general statement is:
  m_N(t) = x_w + 2*3^N*t, so for odd u, m_N mod u determines t mod u (2*3^N invertible).
  Here u=5 and the lift interval has only n=4 < 5 points, so t mod 5 = t,
  which determines t mod 2^K.  The feature is informative ONLY through the truncation.
  Over a COMPLETE cylinder (n a multiple of 2^K) CRT makes t mod u independent of
  t mod 2^K, so the feature is exactly uninformative.  Verified:
     complete cylinders, feature m_N mod 3: max |P(fail|class) - P(fail)| = 0.000e+00
     complete cylinders, feature m_N mod 5: max |P(fail|class) - P(fail)| = 8.750e-01
     complete cylinders, feature m_N mod 7: max |P(fail|class) - P(fail)| = 8.750e-01

==============================================================================
A4. The singleton shell s=N in ShellWeyl's normalization
==============================================================================
  WeylBound U N K C  :=  forall s, K <= s -> sum_{g!=0} ||shellWeyl s g|| <= (C-1)*#shell(s)
     N  #shell(s=N)     r_w(1^N)  =2^(N+1)-1?
     4            1           31         True
     8            1          511         True
    12            1        8,191         True
    16            1      131,071         True
    20            1    2,097,151         True

  Singleton => |shellWeyl(g)|=1 for every g, so sum_{g!=0} = 2^n - 1 with n=s+1-K=N+1-K.
  WeylBound then forces (C-1) >= 2^n - 1, i.e. C >= 2^n = 2^d with d = N+1-K.
    kappa  N=ceil(kB), B=100   K=B  s=N >= K?  d=N+1-K     C >= 2^d
     0.65                 65   100      False      -34          n/a
     0.80                 80   100      False      -19          n/a
     0.95                 95   100      False       -4          n/a
     1.00                100   100       True        1          2^1
     1.05                105   100       True        6          2^6
     1.20                120   100       True       21         2^21

  Also: r_w(1^N) = 2^(N+1)-1 < 2^K iff N+1 <= K, i.e. d <= 0.  So whenever the shell IS
  in scope (d >= 1) the true below-cutoff count is 0 while the main term is #shell/2^n =
  2^-d > 0: the exact signed discrepancy is -2^-d, FAVOURABLE, and destroyed by | . |.
  CONCLUSION: the obstruction is correct mathematics, but it bites only for kappa >= 1.
  At the Phase B target kappa=0.65 the shell s=N has s < K and is out of scope, i.e.
  already fully resolved as a complete cylinder.
